#    DISCARD (direct non-empty). Finding: cellular CGNAT does NOT defeat
#    hole-punching on its own — the dialer behind CGNAT reaches a home NAT that
#    admits the punch (permissive mapping, or iroh's port mapper). The runbook's
#    premise sentence was wrong and is rewritten.
#
# 2. The sender is capped at the bar. RuggedFox's upload capacity to a neutral
#    endpoint (100 MiB POST to https://speed.cloudflare.com/__up), twice:

run1: 2636057 B/s http=200 time=39.778s = 21.1 Mbit/s
run2: 3213088 B/s http=200 time=32.635s = 25.7 Mbit/s

#    The Mac's hotspot DOWNlink, same method (26 MiB GET from Cloudflare):
#    21179400 B/s = 169.4 Mbit/s — not the bottleneck.
#
#    So 22.6 Mbit/s over the punched path IS the home ISP's uplink, and a
#    600 s soak on any path — forced relay included — would file an ISP cap as
#    a transport verdict. Not run. Both directions on this pair are
#    sender-capped: the Mac's cellular UPLINK one way (the 8.7 Mbit/s of
#    relay-leg-TWO-MACHINE-probe.stdout), RuggedFox's home UPLINK the other.
#
# WHAT WOULD MEASURE THE FLOOR: a sender with real uplink — a cloud VM with a
# symmetric >=100 Mbit link — serving to the Mac on the hotspot, with the
# relay FORCED on the Mac (pf block of outbound UDP to the VM's address; a
# public VM punches too), recorded as forced. Different networks stays met.
# RuggedFox has no global IPv6 (only the Tailscale ULA, v6 egress unreachable),
# so a v4-only block is complete against it; check the VM's v6 before ruling.
#
# Harness note (found reading media_bridge_bench.rs:714-715): `fetch_until`
# checks the deadline at the top of each WHOLE-FILE pull, so on a 1 GiB origin
# at ~22 Mbit/s a `--duration-secs 600` run lasts ~760 s (two full pulls).
# `secs` will not read 600; the BAR line still judges >= 600.
#
# 3. Capacity on the DISCARDED (mixed, hole-punched) path — a full 1 GiB pull
#    through the same bridge, labelled by the Mac side for what it is. Not a
#    sample. Best case for the transport, and it sits flat AT the bar:

